-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  Version 0.1.1 (2026-11-27)
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  * The presets' titles fit System Monitor's sidebar at its default width (10 grid units, about 126 pixels of text at
    Noto Sans 10 pt) in the ten languages: the GPU part with this computer's own count ("1 GPU", "2 GPUs") and the
    thread part ("all threads": one chart; "per thread": a chart each). 0.1.0's "Overview: every GPU, many threads"
    was cut to "Overview: every GP…". The every-GPU presets are written only for a computer with two display devices
    or more, so no two presets carry the same title.
  * Each logical processor has its own label, "Thread N" (N its processor number plus one, in the ten languages),
    through the faces' own sensor labels; many threads are a grid of one chart each laid out in the page's rows. 0.1.0
    used KDE's facegrid, whose cells share one configuration group (FaceGrid.qml gives every cell the group name
    "FaceGrid", FaceLoader.cpp builds each cell's controller on it), and on the station two threads showed under one
    name ("Core 3" and "Core 8" twice, no "Core 5" or "Core 9").
  * A one-GPU preset of a computer with no card System Monitor measures names the devices it does not measure beside
    its note.

-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  Version 0.1.0 (2026-11-27)
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  * System Monitor's Overview fits the computer it runs on (queue row 182; Daniel, 2026-10-17: "system monitor
    overview presets for different configs like multiple GPUs and CPUs"; "auto apply by hardware probe"). At a
    person's login the graphics cards are read in ksystemstats 6.7.4's order (PCI classes 0x030000, 0x030200 and
    0x038000 of AMD, NVIDIA and Intel), with what each card's driver reports of usage, video memory, temperature and
    power (AMD: gpu_busy_percent, mem_info_vram_*, the amdgpu hwmon's temp1 and power1; NVIDIA: nvidia-smi dmon, a
    column printed as - is not reported; Intel: usage only, through i915's PMU and ksystemstats_intel_helper's
    CAP_PERFMON or perf_event_paranoid 0), and the processor threads from /proc/cpuinfo.
  * Four presets are written to the person's System Monitor folder: one GPU (the one that draws the desktop) or every
    GPU, few (up to 8) or many processor threads. Every GPU has its usage chart, its name, video memory, temperature
    and power where reported; a sensor its driver does not report is named on the page ("Not reported to System
    Monitor: ...") and never shown, so no zero stands for it. Many threads are a grid of one chart per thread.
  * The first time, the preset of the computer's class is the start page (Ctrl+Alt+Del opens it), KDE's Overview and
    the other presets are listed in Edit Pages, hidden. A person or an administrator who had chosen already, or a
    person who edited KDE's Overview, keeps their arrangement; the other presets are only added hidden. The choice is
    written once: the person's later choice is never written again.
  * When the cards or threads change, the presets the person did not edit or remove are written again for the
    computer; the person's choice stays.
  * A probe that cannot read the computer writes nothing, so KDE's Overview stays, and the person is told why through
    sparky-stereo-problems (LOCAL2, Repair runs the probe again); a later probe that works clears it.
  * The pages' words and the problem in the ten languages (en pl pt_BR fr de es ja ko zh_CN zh_TW).
